## MODULE 4: indicators.py

PURPOSE: All technical indicator calculations. Pure functions only. Input = pd.Series or pd.DataFrame. Output = pd.Series.

```
function calc_sma(series: pd.Series, period: int) -> pd.Series:
    Return series.rolling(period).mean()

function calc_ema(series: pd.Series, period: int) -> pd.Series:
    Return series.ewm(span=period, adjust=False).mean()

function calc_rsi(series: pd.Series, period: int) -> pd.Series:
    delta = series.diff()
    gain  = delta.clip(lower=0).rolling(period).mean()
    loss  = (-delta.clip(upper=0)).rolling(period).mean()
    rs    = gain / (loss + 1e-10)
    return 100 - (100 / (1 + rs))

function calc_atr(high, low, close, period: int) -> pd.Series:
    tr1 = high - low
    tr2 = (high - close.shift(1)).abs()
    tr3 = (low  - close.shift(1)).abs()
    tr  = pd.concat([tr1, tr2, tr3], axis=1).max(axis=1)
    return tr.rolling(period).mean()

function calc_adx(high, low, close, period: int) -> pd.Series:
    Compute +DM, -DM, TR.
    Smooth each with Wilder smoothing (rolling mean approximation is acceptable for scanner).
    +DI = 100 × smoothed_plus_dm / smoothed_tr
    -DI = 100 × smoothed_minus_dm / smoothed_tr
    DX  = 100 × abs(+DI - -DI) / (+DI + -DI + 1e-10)
    ADX = DX.rolling(period).mean()
    Return ADX series.

function calc_obv(close: pd.Series, volume: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:
    direction = sign(close.diff()).fillna(0)
    return (direction × volume).cumsum()

function calc_bb_bandwidth(close: pd.Series, period: int, std_mult: float) -> pd.Series:
    sma   = calc_sma(close, period)
    std   = close.rolling(period).std()
    upper = sma + std_mult × std
    lower = sma - std_mult × std
    return (upper - lower) / (sma + 1e-10)

function calc_all(df: pd.DataFrame, cfg: dict) -> dict:
    Call all above functions.
    Return dict of named Series:
    {
        "sma20", "sma50", "sma200",
        "ema20",
        "rsi",
        "atr", "atr_pct",
        "adx",
        "obv",
        "bb_bw",
    }
    All computed using parameters from cfg.
```

